<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Óscar Romero</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1917">1917</a>–<a href="/years/1980">1980</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Saint Óscar Romero</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Óscar Romero was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-salvador">San Salvador</a> from 1977 to 1980. Initially considered a conservative, he became an outspoken critic of military repression and death squads, using his radio sermons to denounce killings. He was shot dead while celebrating mass on 24 March 1980, a murder that the UN Truth Commission attributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roberto-daubuisson">Roberto D'Aubuisson</a>. He was canonised in 2018.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oscar-romero.jpg" alt="Óscar Romero" width="320" height="364" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arzobispado de San Salvador; Congregatio de Causis Sanctorum · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monse%C3%B1or_Romero_(colour).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
